const subnav = [
  { href: '/community/events', label: 'Events' },
  { href: '/community/chapters', label: 'Chapters' },
  { href: '/community/insights', label: 'Insights' },
];

const toc = [
  { href: '#why-slow', label: 'Why go slowly', active: true },
  { href: '#weights', label: 'Validator weights' },
  { href: '#clip', label: 'The clip step' },
  { href: '#bonds', label: 'Bonds & trust' },
  { href: '#emission', label: 'Emission split' },
  { href: '#what-if', label: 'What if a validator lies?' },
  { href: '#what-next', label: 'What to read next' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Yuma Consensus, slowly — Insights — OpenTAO"
  description="A hand-traced walk through one block of Yuma consensus. By the end you can predict what every validator's weight does to emission."
  section="community"
>
  <SubNav items={subnav} active="/community/insights" rightMeta="22 min · published 2026·05·19" />

  <article>
    <section class="border-b hairline">
      <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 pt-14 pb-10">
        <Breadcrumb crumbs={[
          { href: '/', label: 'OpenTAO' },
          { href: '/community/events', label: 'Community' },
          { href: '/community/insights', label: 'Insights' },
          { label: 'Yuma walk-through' },
        ]} />

        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="section-label" style="color: #b8470b;">Deep dive</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)]">22 min read</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)]">·  2026·05·19</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)]">·  updated 2026·05·22</span>
          <span class="ml-auto text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] hidden md:flex items-center gap-3"><a href="#" class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)]">EN</a> · <a href="#" class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)]">中</a></span>
        </div>

        <h1 class="text-[44px] md:text-[64px] leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.025em] font-semibold max-w-[920px] mb-6">
          <span class="font-serif italic font-normal">Yuma</span> consensus, slowly:<br>a hand-traced walk through one block.
        </h1>

        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-8">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #6741d9, #1c6ea4);"></div>
          <div>
            <div class="text-sm">Rho Carter <span class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] font-mono text-xs">· @rho.lab</span></div>
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)]">core dev contributor · translation by @yuki</div>
          </div>
          <span class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-3 text-xs">
            <button class="px-3 py-1.5 border hairline rounded text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)] hover:border-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Copy link</button>
            <button class="px-3 py-1.5 border hairline rounded text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)] hover:border-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Tweet</button>
            <button class="px-3 py-1.5 border hairline rounded text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)] hover:border-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">PDF</button>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 py-16">
        <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[180px_1fr] gap-12">

          <aside class="hidden lg:block sticky top-24 self-start">
            <div class="section-label mb-3">On this page</div>
            {toc.map((t) => (
              <a href={t.href} class={`toc-link ${t.active ? 'active' : ''}`}>{t.label}</a>
            ))}
          </aside>

          <div class="prose">

            <p class="lead">If you read the Yuma paper and felt it was hiding something, you're not wrong. It's not malicious — the math is fine — but the paper writes for a reviewer, not a builder. Let's walk a single block by hand. By the end you'll predict what every validator's weight does to emission, without opening the paper again.</p>

            <h2 id="why-slow">Why go slowly</h2>
            <p>Most subnet engineers learn Yuma the wrong way: they implement the validator code, hit production, watch their emission, and back-fit intuition. That works, but the intuition is shaped by the bugs they happened to encounter. This piece reverses the order — we'll pin down the mechanism first, then the bugs will look like obvious special cases.</p>
            <p>Setup. We're on a fictional subnet with five miners (M1–M5) and three validators (V1–V3). One block. No history. Bonds start at zero. Everyone is honest. We'll see why even with honest actors the rankings <em>still</em> compress, and why that's the whole point.</p>

            <blockquote>If you remember one thing: Yuma is not "validators score miners". It is "validators score miners <em>and validators score each other through which miners they agree on</em>". The second clause is where all the interesting behavior lives.</blockquote>

            <h2 id="weights">Validator weights</h2>
            <p>Each validator submits a vector of weights — one number per miner — that they think reflects how much emission each miner deserves. Below is the toy table we'll walk through:</p>

            <figure class="chart not-prose">
              <div class="grid grid-cols-6 gap-2 text-center text-xs font-mono">
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2">weights</div>
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2 border-l hairline">M1</div>
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2 border-l hairline">M2</div>
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2 border-l hairline">M3</div>
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2 border-l hairline">M4</div>
                <div class="text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] p-2 border-l hairline">M5</div>

                <div class="p-2" style="color: #b8470b;">V1</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.40</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.30</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.15</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.10</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.05</div>

                <div class="p-2" style="color: #1c6ea4;">V2</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.35</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.35</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.20</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.08</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.02</div>

                <div class="p-2" style="color: #15803d;">V3</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.45</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.20</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.20</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.10</div>
                <div class="p-2 border-l hairline border-t">0.05</div>
              </div>
              <figcaption>fig 1 · validator weight vectors for one block</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>Three validators, three slightly different opinions about who's good. V1 thinks M1 is clearly the best (40%). V2 thinks M1 and M2 are tied. V3 likes M1 the most by margin. In the real world these vectors arrive in the same block.</p>

            <h2 id="clip">The clip step</h2>
            <p>Here's where most explanations skip. Yuma doesn't just average the validators' weights. It computes — for each miner — the <em>median</em> weight across all validators, then clips each validator's weight to be at most that median. The word "clip" is doing work here. Specifically:</p>
            <pre>for each miner m:
  median[m] = median(weight[v][m] for v in validators)
for each validator v:
  for each miner m:
    weight[v][m] = min(weight[v][m], median[m])</pre>

            <p>What does the clip <em>do</em>? Run it on M1 with our data: median of (0.40, 0.35, 0.45) is 0.40. V3's value 0.45 gets clipped down to 0.40. The runaway voter doesn't get to push the value up by themselves.</p>

            <p>This is the consensus mechanism. Stake-weighted voting that says: if you want a miner to get more weight, you can't act alone — you need at least half the validator stake to back you. Pre-clip excess is wasted.</p>

            <div class="pull">Yuma is not democracy. It is a stake-weighted median, then everyone clipped down to it. Outliers cost nothing — but they buy nothing either.</div>

            <h2 id="bonds">Bonds &amp; trust</h2>
            <p>After the clip, each validator has a (possibly-clipped) weight vector. Now we compute <strong>bonds</strong> — how much each validator's view "aligns" with each miner. The intuition: a validator who keeps backing miners that the rest of the network <em>also</em> ends up backing accumulates trust over time. That trust pays back as a share of future emission.</p>

            <p>Bonds are bond-EMA — exponentially weighted moving average of the post-clip weights. The decay constant matters a lot. With a high decay, a validator who shifts opinion fast loses bonds fast. With a low decay, bonds are sticky and validators who learned in week one keep earning from week ten.</p>

            <p>We won't grind through every cell of the bond update, but the shape is:</p>
            <pre>bond_new[v][m] = (1 - α) · bond_old[v][m] + α · post_clip_weight[v][m]</pre>

            <p>where <code>α ≈ 0.1</code> on most subnets. <a href="#" class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">SN42 runs α=0.06</a>, which is why their validator rankings are unusually slow to react to incumbent miner failures.</p>

            <h2 id="emission">Emission split</h2>
            <p>Finally, emission per block (let's call it E) is split. Half goes to miners, half to validators — that's a subnet hyperparameter and is set per-subnet. Inside each half, the split follows post-clip weight (for miners) and bond-weighted contribution (for validators).</p>

            <p>Walking our example:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Post-clip weight column-sums give miner shares.</li>
              <li>Validator-side: each validator's share is proportional to <code>Σ bond · post_clip_weight</code> over the miners they bonded to.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>This means a validator who held a strong bond to M1 — and was right that M1 deserved high post-clip weight — gets paid more <em>this</em> block. And the bond they're building keeps paying in subsequent blocks, until decayed out.</p>

            <h2 id="what-if">What if a validator lies?</h2>
            <p>Let's say V3 in our example tries to inflate M5 (their wallet's hot key, perhaps). They submit 0.50 instead of 0.05. Two things happen:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>The clip swallows it.</strong> Median of (0.05, 0.02, 0.50) is 0.05. V3's vote on M5 gets clipped from 0.50 down to 0.05. Their attempt to inflate M5 has zero effect on emission.</li>
              <li><strong>Their bond suffers.</strong> Across other miners they didn't change, V3 still bonded to miners that the network agreed on. But the "useless" vote on M5 contributes nothing to bond accumulation. Over many blocks, V3 falls behind validators who didn't waste their attention.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>So Yuma penalizes lying by ignoring it, not by punishing it. There's no slashing — just opportunity cost. Whether that's enough deterrent depends on whether the validator could have made more money being honest. In current Bittensor mainnet, the answer is overwhelmingly yes.</p>

            <h2 id="what-next">What to read next</h2>
            <p>The compression we just walked through is the source of every interesting subnet behavior — drift, lock-in, miner specialization. If you want to dig further:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>For the math: the <a href="#">Bittensor whitepaper</a> formalizes everything above as a fixed-point iteration.</li>
              <li>For practice: Reza's <a href="/community/insights">"Tuning a validator's bonding parameters"</a> walks through how to set α on your own subnet.</li>
              <li>For the concept page: see <a href="/beginner/concepts/yuma-consensus">Yuma Consensus</a> in the Wiki.</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="footnote mt-12">Last updated 2026·05·22. Caught an error? <a href="#" class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">Edit this page on GitHub</a>. Translations: <a href="#" class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">中文 by @yuki</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <section class="border-t hairline bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
    <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 py-12">
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] gap-8 items-center">
        <div class="author-card">
          <div class="av"></div>
          <div>
            <div class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] font-mono mb-1">Written by</div>
            <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Rho Carter <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)]">@rho.lab</span></h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-[480px]">Core protocol contributor since 2024. Writes about mechanism design and the parts of the codebase nobody else wants to document. Berlin-based.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-end gap-2 text-xs">
          <a href="#" class="font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)]">All work by Rho Carter →</a>
          <a href="#" class="font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-fg)]">Follow on Twitter</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t hairline">
    <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 py-14">
      <div class="section-label mb-3">Keep reading</div>
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-8">If this resonated, read these next.</h3>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <a href="#" class="card rounded-md p-6 block">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] mb-3 block">Tutorial · 18 min</span>
          <h4 class="font-serif italic text-lg leading-snug mb-2">Tuning a validator's bonding parameters without getting penalized.</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Practical follow-up to this piece — the α you set determines everything.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="#" class="card rounded-md p-6 block">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] mb-3 block">Deep dive · 25 min</span>
          <h4 class="font-serif italic text-lg leading-snug mb-2">Designing a subnet's scoring function: a field guide.</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">If you're building, this is the next thing to read.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="#" class="card rounded-md p-6 block">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-[color:var(--color-fg-dim)] mb-3 block">Retrospective · 14 min</span>
          <h4 class="font-serif italic text-lg leading-snug mb-2">Why dTAO changed everything (and three things it didn't).</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Where the consensus rules touch market dynamics — a year-one retrospective.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t hairline">
    <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 py-10 grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] gap-6 items-center">
      <div>
        <div class="section-label mb-2">Discussion</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Comments aren't on the page — the conversation lives on Discord.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <a href="#" class="px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium rounded-md border hairline-strong hover:border-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Discuss on Discord →</a>
        <a href="#" class="px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium rounded-md border hairline-strong hover:border-[color:var(--color-fg-muted)]">Cite this article</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
